   100	### 1.3 Blind matter census (articles only; frozen before any send)
   101	
   102	1. **Sample (D668).** Every kept article in the scored window 2026-08-31 to 09-20, read in batches of about 40 per session. Each family merges across every batch and week before reconciling. The warm-up is 07-01 to 08-30, unfiltered: the exclusion filter applies to the scored window only. Read with week reading A1's
   103	   brief (`../2026-11-14-use-acceptance/week/brief-A1.md`), unchanged except for the file names.
   104	2. **Readers.** Codex (memories off, closed, D661) and Claude (closed command line), apart. Nine weeks × 2 families, in fresh sessions.
   105	3. **Merge.** One matter is one bet. Two entries are one matter only when the same outcome settles both and their answers cannot move apart.
   106	   Another year of a figure is another matter.
   107	   - Each family merges its own nine lists in one session.
   108	   - Two reconcilers, one per family, map every matter across the two families' lists (`same` or `none`). A matter is listed by both when
   109	     both reconcilers say `same`.
   110	   - A one-family matter goes to two third readers (one per family) with its articles. It joins the list when both say it is worth a question.
   111	4. **The company-figure flag.** Each family marks every listed matter `company figure` or not, using D505's own list: 营收、毛利率的指引、市场预期、实际业绩.
   112	   The flag is set when both say yes; a split is listed.
   113	5. **N** is every listed matter (D665). The flagged subgroup is printed beside the verdict with its own a, b and N, because by D505 the new
   114	   system does not open such questions. Alex sees how much of any gap that rule makes. (D431: the comparison is of the products as
   115	   configured.)
   116	6. **Frozen:** `matters.json`. Each entry holds the matter, an example question, its source articles, its first source day and its flag.
   117	7. **Too small at once:** if N < 20, the verdict is "too small" and goes to Alex before any paid send.
   118	
   119	### 1.4 The verdict rule (`verdict-rule.json`, hashed; `score_comparison.py` with a test and a mutant per rule)
   120	
   121	1. **The cutoff:** one for every matter, the end of the invocation's last day (09-20 in the full run). Libraries are read as they stand then.
   122	   - Closed questions still count.
   123	   - Timeliness is not scored. The days from first source to opening are printed beside.
   124	2. **Covers (mapping, A2's form, blind):**
   125	   - **The rule:** both families mark the question `opened`, or `narrower` with both saying it carries the matter's main outcome
   126	     (ACCEPTANCE-DESIGN A; `tally_week.covered`). `broader` and `missed` do not cover.
   127	   - **Blinding:** the readers see each draw's library under a random X/Y label, the key outside the repository, and are told to ignore
   128	     style.
   129	   - **Disagreements:** two third readers, one per family. A remaining split is resolved per counting (item 7, order: covers > does not
   130	     cover).
   131	3. **Same question:**
